From f88973c6fd24138f369ff4c80b15dfd5eb3131e4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 23:11:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(fairness): persist observation identities and writer
 clocks [daveey]

Why: every exported DB row previously lacked source and clock evidence, so
otherwise-passing fairness gates were always indeterminate. Owner history
also used the unrelated metadata bound and could reject complete journals.

Persist observation IDs transactionally at the source journal, not at drain.
Add opt-in writer-side wall/monotonic samples with arm-time declared UTC
uncertainty and preserve legacy unknowns. Register the SQLite sampler on
tracked connections. Require explicit --db for clock-enabled capture and
document old/raw-writer incompatibility and clear/reconnect recovery; do not
activate it automatically on shared live databases. The metric kernel must
not narrow persisted uncertainty to a smaller manifest assumption.

Read the complete owner journal up to a separate 64 MiB bound; never drop
its early coverage by tail truncation. Keep the assembled capture bound.

Proof: canonical run_tests.sh, 622 passed / 0 failed across 33 files.
scripts/check --base origin/main: 21 checks passed.
Seven exact-anchor mutants killed: source ID removal, drain-time clock,
missing opening trigger, wrong owner limit, narrowed clock uncertainty,
missing reopen registration, malformed clock accepted.
Initial regression was red for unsupported clock_error_ns; live one-hour
acceptance is intentionally still outstanding, not replaced with fixtures.
